in england we will remove all remaining  domestic restrictions a lot of us are  leading much more normal lives perhaps  most normal now for the last couple of  years during the pandemic but the latest  results from the office for national  statistics show kovid really hasn't gone  away  [Music]  in the week up to march the 12th three  and a quarter million people across the  uk had the disease that's about 4.8  percent of the population and it is an  upward trend that's the concern for many  people  if you look what's happening since the  omicron peaked in the beginning of  january when about 6.8 percent of the  population had the disease since then  cases have been coming down to about 3.8  at the end of february but since then  they've been going up  there are many different reasons you can  look at the lifting of plan b at the end  of january you can look at the prime  minister's statement that we would be  living with kovid in future and that has  led to a big change in behavior people  are more relaxed the wearing a face mask  the use of lateral flows social  distancing all these things have been  decreasing in recent weeks and people  are mixing more with others both  socially and and at work and that of  course gives an opportunity for the  virus to spread  there will be new variants and it is  very possible  that those will be worse than omicron  the big difference is that we're now  dealing with a slightly different virus  for the first few months of this year  omicron has been dominant it's now  called ba 0.1 but that's now being  overtaken by a very similar virus called  ba 0.2  the two of them are so similar that  they're called sister variants or  sub-variants and they are both just as  sensitive to the vaccine they both cause  the same very mild disease  but the point is that ba2 is 1.4 times  more infectious and that gives it the  edge over the original omicron virus and  allows it to spread much more quickly uh  there are sort of so-called sub-variants  of omicron and we're not concerned about  any of those at this important time  there has been some talk of this delta  cron variant it's a hybrid if you like  of two different viruses the original  delta and omicron that have come  together probably in one person who's  been infected with both viruses at the  same time that has been an issue in  france but the health secretary sergey  javid has said there have only been a  handful of cases here in the uk and at  the moment there's no clear evidence  that it's any more of a threat than  omicron itself and of course delta  was effectively dealt with by the  vaccine  the situation in hong kong is certainly  a concern but it's very different to  what we're dealing with here in the uk  in hong kong vaccination rates of the  elderly are much lower it's only about  one in three  people over the age of 80  have actually had the vaccine so the  most vulnerable are still unprotected  against the virus which is spreading  around there hong kong disappointingly  no problem with vaccine supply  for well over a year  had not achieved coverage of our most  vulnerable group the second point is  that in hong kong they use the chinese  sinovac vaccine and there has been a  great deal of concern about just how  good that is  and the chinese are understood to be  looking at mrna vaccines developing  their own because those have really  stood out in this pandemic as the best  way of protecting people against the  rapidly evolving virus we need to find  our own way out of this epidemic it has  outgrown our capacity it's unlikely that  here in the uk we would suddenly be  developing a situation the dreadful  situation that they're encountering in  hong kong where where some say that  they're actually beginning to run out of  coffins because deaths are so high  hospital admissions in the uk are also  rising and many people are concerned  about that the rise in hospital  admissions aligns very precisely with  the rise in cases both started to take  off  at the end of february now in previous  waves you would have had a lag between  people getting infected and people  getting so ill that they needed  emergency treatment this time that  hasn't happened they're both happening  at the same time and that suggests the  rise in hospital admissions is actually  due to people coming into hospital with  the virus but are being treated for  other conditions they're not coming in  for treatment for covid itself it's an  important distinction so although  hospitals will be disrupted by the rise  in emissions it doesn't mean that we're  going backwards and that as a nation  we're suffering more severe disease  quite firmly the answer is no this is a  very different situation the vaccine is  still highly effective the immunity is  being topped up as the virus spreads  and the two together are in a very  effective way of controlling the spread  of the virus it's very likely that as  the weather warms up and we spend more  time outside that that too will have an  effect on transmission but that doesn't  mean omicron has gone away it may well  return in the autumn as the effect of  the boosters does begin to wear off most  important thing you can do is get your  booster we've already heard that the  government is planning some kind of  booster campaign in the autumn months  just to make sure that we are topped up  and that we can go into the winter  hopefully better prepared than we have  been in recent years
 

